Z||000010||Jaei Sun||08-19-2000||03:50 AM||||Here are magazine scans of 2 different articles in Kit Car Magazine located on these pages.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fierodrivers.com/magazines/kit_car/july_98/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierodrivers.com/magazines/kit_car/july_98/index.html</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fierodrivers.com/magazines/kit_car/may_99/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierodrivers.com/magazines/kit_car/may_99/index.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Jaei Sun (edited 08-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Chris McDonald||08-19-2000||07:50 AM||||The Northstar does appear to be the ultimate way to gain more power in a "factory" type package.  However, considering the cost and complications involved you may not be as interested as originally thought.  <P>There is a fellow in my town that has a fast back 86 GT with the Northstar package and nitrous.  He spent $12k on the conversion including the engine and installation kit.  I can't remember what brand kit he is using.<P>Anyway, here is the story.  He has an ongoing problem getting the transmission to shift in a sporty fashion.  At the $12k price, it did not include any provisions to hook up the a/c system ( a real bummer in Florida).  He ran the car in the 1/4 mile and squeezed low 13 second runs until he broke an axle. He thinks the luxary soft shift points on the trans was slowing him down. He has also been experiencing heat problems in the trunk to the extent that he can't keep anything back there without melting.  The computer has proven to be a real trick.  He is looking for the right company to help him reprogram the module.   <P>It is hard to tell if he is happy with the cost involved to put a light weight 300 hsp V8 in the car.  Not only that, my old Chevy V8 Fiero (See BBBADGT for details) would pull his headlights out from red light to red light.  Proof that torque is your best friend on the street.<P>However, my final word on the subject is that there is no doubt the Northstar just looks like home in the back of the Fiero.  Very smooth and high tech.  If you have the $$$$ to spend, I say go for it.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Dan86gt3.8||08-19-2000||11:26 AM||||I know tulsa, ok is a bit far from southern florida but you ought to check out ed curtis at <A HREF="http://www.fastchip.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fastchip.com/</A>  He is suposedly the best tuner of gm cars and trucks in the intire us.  All the guys that swap heads and go with huge cams in thier f-bods are using this guy.  He is supposed to be real real good.  He recently just got 491 rwhp out of a single turbo ls1..... has your friend checked with design one?  Or will they not give you any help unless you buy the kit from them?||||reg||1
Z||000013||ezsurf||08-19-2000||12:12 PM||||Fierocious,<BR>FYI I wanted to put a Northstar in an 88 coupe.  As I looked into the project, I found out that a Design One conversion kit costs $5000.00.  That's just for the conversion kit.  You then have to spend another $5000.00 for the engine AND transmission.  That amount doesn't get it in the car, that just gets you the parts (not including the car).  Then you have to put it in.  A chevy 350 adds 150lbs to the weight of the car.  A Northstar is aluminum, but you need the engine/transmission package which adds 100lbs overall.  For the money, your much better off with a chevy 350 with a carb setup.  The load carrying capacity of the Fiero is around 400lbs.  If your going to put a V-8 in anything, I would definately add stiffer springs. Just my 2cts. - ez||||reg||1
Z||000014||HYPTNOTiSE||08-19-2000||01:34 PM||||I think that if you put a northstar in a fiero, it's not all that great. It's a great engine in a cadaliac.  It's awesome, but in a fiero, I have only seen 1 person actually do it right.  Jon norman, and no one on this list wants to even conceive the kind of money he put into his car. IT's atronomical.  Another guy who did along with jon, has a purple car with a crazy homemade body kit on it. He went as far as to use most of the cadaliac's engine cradle. He and john had special chips made for they're cars, and you'll never see anymore, ever. GM put a stop to that. The northstar is supposed to be an elusive engine right?  The guy with the purple car said that when his car runs right it's unbelievable, but when it doesn't it doesn't. And usually it doesn't. Chris moores car is nicely done, but i want to see dyno numbers. I'd be extrememly suprised if that northstar is running up to it's potential.  They're so persicikity than unless you have tons of money to dump off to a tuner that wants to do something illegal, they'res no point. Because for the money invested, you could have spent half of that and had a really really bad all aluminum small block chevy. I think that i saw an add for like 4000 you can get a rebuilt 383 stroker making like 450-500hp, and it comes with a 20K mile warranty. That would whoop the pants off a northstar. But i must agree....That engine looks right at home in that engine bay.<P>I'll race chris moore in 2 months, i bet i win.<P><P>------------------<BR>       Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ Nitrous<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/hypsig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Pontiaddict||08-19-2000||03:55 PM||||Northstar computers have a speed limiter in them also. It usually slows the car down before the end of the 1/4 mile. That's the main reason John Norman put a chip in his car (the first anyway).<P>I've been thinking about a 4.9 caddy.<BR>compared to the 2.8<BR>+60 hp +105 lbft +only adds like 15lbs<BR>-No aftermarket parts for it -most people are unsure of it's reliability (Yes 4.1s were junk, but they did improve after the years, How much is still not known.)<BR> <BR>You can do the whole for under a grand if you shop around and do the work yourself.<P>It's not a Northstar but it's a really easy way to get an all aluminum v8 in your car.||||reg||1
Z||000016||rockcrawl||08-19-2000||04:22 PM||||sorry, double post!<p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 08-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||rockcrawl||08-19-2000||04:23 PM||||Wow,<BR> Last time I made a post about the Northstar not being all it's cracked up to be I really got flamed! Now everyone seems to agree. I'm currently doing a 4.9 conversion. 250hp, 275lb/ft torque, and about the same weight as the 2.8. The 4.9 is a very reliable engine, as is the earlier 4.5 PFI. They don't have the problems that haunted the 4.1. <P> The Northstar is impressive if you don't mind spending double the value of your Fiero for an engine swap. I remember seeing one in the back of a street rod and the guy could barely keep it running, and another in a Lambo clone with three radiators and about six fans, and it still overheated, because the engine wasn't tuned right. Doesn't sound like fun to me. <P>That said, I still wouldn't pass one up if the price was right!<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000018||olympic||08-19-2000||04:27 PM||||I think the biggest problem would be the computer.  The Fiero doesn't have ABS, air bags and a bunch of other stuff the Northstar computer is expecting to control or monitor.<P>   As for lazy shift points, there are stand alone tranny control modules that allow you to do whatever you want with the shift points.<P>  If I were attempting this swap and wanted to do it 100% right I'd probably cough up for a custom engine management system like an Electromotive or Haltec.  That way you have complete control over the engine and wont have to deal with the factory computer or OBD II or anything else.  Hooking the engine to a 5 speed would be easier and you could reprogram the computer to adapt to mods.<P>   As for the older Caddy 4.1 and 4.9 I believe they were aluminum block /iron head engines.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Phil||08-20-2000||07:03 AM||||Had a chance to compare a SC3800 and a N* on the dyno  N* was ahead about 15HP 3800 was ahead about 17 Ft-lb.  I can't see much advantage on the N*||||reg||1
Z||000020||HYPTNOTiSE||08-20-2000||11:12 AM||||I don't think a haltech, electromotive, accel dfi, speed pro system, or any other stand alone computer system could handle how complicated the northstar is.  If so everyone with a northsttar would do this.  That would be the easy and inexpensive way to go. <P>The northstar has something nuts like 4 cam sensors, 1 or 2 crank sensors, and lord knows what else. ||||reg||1
Z||000021||stimpy||08-20-2000||01:16 PM||||I have been disappointed by something I heard about DesignOne... a guy in our club has been waiting 16 months for his northstar conversion to be completed, I don't know if it's engine problems or just the fact that other customers cars are taking priority, but if it was my car, I'd be pissed bigtime.||||reg||1
Z||000022||FIEROCIOUS||08-20-2000||03:57 PM||||Thanks for the pro/cons on the engine swap it makes me deffinatly think twice!<P>First off though: I will not buy a 12000.00 dollar kit I could make for 100.00 bucks!<BR>Come on realy Design One is ripping people off!<BR>They don't sell their "parts" for their kit they only do installs! So this tells me they are looking for suckers with money! <P>If I had more money than sence I wouldn't drive a fiero!<P>I can get the engine/tranny/computer for 3000.00 (3000.00 is average around here)<P>I am looking into accels EFI and electromotives tec II systems at this time. An alternative is to have Howell Engine Development make a stand alone engine harness that make the Northstar use engine perameters the fiero can provide. Superchips make the chip to remove the speed limiter.<BR>Any reflections of the engine wireing would be appreciated!<P>2.8=350 lbs.<BR>4.6=403 lbs.<BR>Thats all acessories on the motors I only plan to have an alternator. No AC, no powersteering (It's a sports car) if I wanted luxury I'd buy the Cadillac the Northstar came in! I've already removed 50+ lbs. of weight off the rear already so, I'm ahead of the game!<BR>So were looking at approx. 375 lbs. I have coilovers already so 50.00 later I have the right springs! Happy day!<P>I need info on what they are useing for for brakes and steering knuckles! I wan't to maintain useing the baretta brakes.<BR>Also info on the cooling system and how to center to engine in the cradle could be nice.<P>With all the swaps these engines are begineing to be a part of it is only a matter of time it becomes a common swap!<P>If more people perform this swap the information will soon become more commonplace and (we all) will benifite from others trials!<P>Thanks all! Keep the pro's and Con's com'in!<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000023||lou_dias||08-21-2000||01:05 AM||||Wouldn't a turbo 3.4 make like 300hp?<BR>$3800 for turbo<BR>$1800 for rebuilt 3.4-&gt;3.5<BR>throw in some ported heads and intake, an exhaust and you can have 300hp for under $7k total and keep A/C...||||reg||1
Z||000024||3400Fiero||08-21-2000||10:07 AM||||A Turbo 3400 DOHC is what I have in mind eventually.  I think that would be a very good setup - 350-400 hp running 8-10 pounds of boost.  Obviously an aftermarket computer such as a motec or FJOInc 341B (check out fjoinc.com) would be a necessity!<P>------------------<BR>1988 Fiero 3400DOHC<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000025||88 Fiero||08-21-2000||11:15 AM||||If you guys are interested in turbo charging a 3400 DOHC...contact Chris West at West Coast Fieros...His 3400 DOHC is turbo charge and he is using the BUICK Grand National Turbo Unit... I heard it's very very wicked... Also Bob York has a 3.4 V6 that is also turbo charged with a Intercooler and automatic...and alot of people atest that his car can be a good competition against a V8 and he is only running 7 lbs of boost...He is planning to cranck it around 10 to 12 lbs of boost....||||reg||1
Z||000026||88 Fiero||08-21-2000||11:15 AM||||If you guys are interested in turbo charging a 3400 DOHC...contact Chris West at West Coast Fieros...His 3400 DOHC is turbo charge and he is using the BUICK Grand National Turbo Unit... I heard it's very very wicked... Also Bob York has a 3.4 V6 that is also turbo charged with a Intercooler and automatic...and alot of people atest that his car can be a good competition against a V8 and he is only running 7 lbs of boost...He is planning to cranck it around 10 to 12 lbs of boost....||||reg||1
Z||000027||FIEROCIOUS||08-27-2000||06:49 PM||||Does Chris Moore have an e-mail address?<BR>tgowens?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000028||Old Lar||08-27-2000||08:14 PM||||I love all the shouldas for cost. You'll need to find out the done thats to get your final costs.<P>Everything I've ever seen has cost more that you would expect.  The days of $100 or $1000 engine swaps went out 30 years ago.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Philphine||08-27-2000||10:32 PM||||rockcrawl, i thought the pre northstar 4.9 was only good for 200hp have you found some after maket pieces or modded stock pieces. 250 horses would make it much more desireable.||||reg||1
Z||000030||FIEROCIOUS||08-28-2000||01:38 AM||||Well, as I figure it this is what I "estimate" the price of the convertion.<P>1. Engine cradle and motor mount modification: 50.00-100.00<BR>2. High volume fuel pump: 40.00-130.00<BR>3. Axles modified: 100.00-150.00<BR>4. Exhaust done by me: 100.00-200.00<BR>5. Air cleaner tube: 5.00<BR>6. Torque strut brackets: 30.00-50.00<BR>7. Pro-stick B&M shifter: 200.00<BR>8. ECM harness: don't know yet they never wrote me back!<BR>9. Prom chip: 159.00 (speed limit removed)<BR>10. Radiator: don't know yet depends on how I do it?<BR>11. Modify the trunk for exhaust: 30.00<BR>12. Decklid struts: 55.00<BR>13. Idle speed brackets: ?<BR>14. Surge tank: ?<BR>15. Tranny cooler: 75.00<BR>16. Oil cooler: 75.00<P>Thats what I know I need add in a 3,000.00 engine tranny and PCM and you have a total plus slap a grand on for good measure! That's a realistic estimate. So you have a complete swap for 4919.00 minimum wich is around what you would pay for Ed Parks to slap a 3.4 in your ride! You just have a spare 100 HP sittin around for "just in case"<BR>wink! wink!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000031||Monkeyman||08-28-2000||07:38 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by HYPTNOTiSE:<BR><B>It's awesome, but in a fiero, I have only seen 1 person actually do it right.  Jon norman, and no one on this list wants to even conceive the kind of money he put into his car. IT's atronomical.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm guessing John has about 20G wrapped up in his.  Of course, that would include body, suspension and interior mods.  On the other hand, John did all the work himself and saved a ton of money on labor.  If I had to venture a guess, I'd say it would take $35-$40 thousand to duplicate Johns car.  (He doesn't call it "Obsession" for nothing! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000032||HYPTNOTiSE||08-28-2000||09:00 AM||||High volume fuel pump: minimum of 100 dollars.<P>Cradle bushings by them selves if going into a pre 88 fiero are 150 dollars. Then you can add on the cost of motor mount mods. <P>You can expect 300 dollars for a radiator.<P>And then expect to tune and play with the computer for 2 years after the project to get engine codes to go away. And since it's OBD-2 it's not just a simple task of disconnecting the battery, you have to get a code reader and remove the codes manually. Well thats after the problems fixed. <P>After you're done i'll race you...<BR><P>------------------<BR>       Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ Nitrous<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/hypsig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000033||FIEROCIOUS||08-28-2000||02:57 PM||||Alright Hyptno, Terryk gave me a tip about a fuel pump for a turbo TA that can be used if you didn't already know I am the parts manager of Pepboys so I think I know the price!<P>I already have cradle bushings and they cost me a wopping 45.00 from energy suspention. As for a radiator I need one from an automatic but I will soon have a parts car that will have one. I felt it would be a waste of money to buy a griffin radiator when if I realy need better cooling I can use waterless coolant or water wetter from redline. Most people use the stock radiator for LT1 convertions and with the Northstar being aluminum should run cooler.<P>And last but not least I will be useing a 95 engine and computer non OBD-2 stand-alone settup. Again I don't know the price of the harness yet. <P>Hyptno, remember to add 1000.00 for good measure if I run into problems. If I wasn't realistic I would have said 3919.00!<P>Oh and Hyptno, you're on! Like you said though I need a year to get the squirels chased out!<P>If someone has thought of details I've left out please contribute I need all the info I can get. Thanks!<p>[This message has been edited by FIEROCIOUS (edited 08-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000034||terryk||08-28-2000||03:35 PM||||There is a non-ODB II ECM for the NS. I think in 94. I did a bit of work on it a while back.<P>Fierocious, check into the 92-95 Bonneville SSEi VIN 1 fuel pump too. I'll bet it's far more expensive. Should be a big beast. ||||reg||1
Z||000035||Shaun41178||08-28-2000||10:22 PM||||Fierocious, you say you have a built 2.8 making about 190 hp.  If that is the case then a superchager is going to make more then 50 hp if you put it on.  50 hp is bout 6 psi of boost.  If your 2.8 is built like you say it is you can easily run 10-12 psi making about 100 hp extra or so which would put you around 300 at the crank or roughly 250 at the wheels.<P>Since you say you want to do a northstar I think I have the proper impression that you don't have too many reserves about the cost.  Supercharger is as you know $3k and doesn't add any extra engine compartment heat. <P>THought about nitrous?  Pretty cheap for a kit at around $500 or so and is "on demand" only so you only have to punch it when the demand arises around the pesky C5 vettes.  110 hp from a nitrous kit will put you near 300 hp as well.  I am telling you 300 crank hp in a fiero is downright fast.  I dynoed 180 at the wheels with 230 torque at the wheels and I must admit my car is a fun ride.  With that little power I am able to run low 14 sec qtr times and get to 60 in under 6 seconds(5.6) with an auto tranny.  Oh yea that is with my 2.8 as I have yet to get my 3.2 in.  With my 3.2 and about 70 extra hp at the wheels I think I can pull sub 5 sec to 60 times and high 12 qtr mile times.  Pretty darn fast if you ask me.  I mean right now with my 2.8 I am outrunning many a mustang on the street and hang with Camaros and Firebirds and have beaten a few vettes albeit older ones(89 year)<P>i think if you go ahead with a northstar that would be pretty cool.  But be prepared for unexpected troubles.  Oh yea, I know Ed Parks had a Northstar and tranny already mounted on a cradle down there at his place that he was looking to sell for like $3500 I think.  I would send him an email to see if he still has it as that could save you some time with mounting it to the cradle and getting brackets and all that done up.<P>Oh yea if you do get the northstar in there, I challenge you as well like Hyptnotise.  This could start to get interesting  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>3.2 With nitrous<BR>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178||||reg||1
Z||000036||BBBAD GT||08-28-2000||11:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Shaun41178:<BR><B>Supercharger is as you know $3k and doesn't add any extra engine compartment heat</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Which blower are you refering to that doesn't add any extra heat?  You can cook an egg on my Vortech after just tooling around town.  If it isn't creating heat...it isn't working.<P><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">    <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000037||HYPTNOTiSE||08-29-2000||12:25 AM||||It's on shaun.  It's smack down time. Who else wants to race?  We'll have to scedule a race at a nice track and get it all on video so that we can post the results with living proof of me spanking your ass, buddy!(had to add the comma i know it isn't proper grammar but i didn't want people to think i was spanking shauns gay friend/ass buddy.) <P>    If you guys wanna save money,  I know a great place, i dont wanna waste money at the track, lets go to a crowded street with lots of pedestrians, and see who makes it the farthest fastest without seriously injuring someone, broken appendages are ok, but severe internal bleeding is defiantly a disqualification.  <P>Wow, I'm really tired sorry for the lude and crude comments. ||||reg||1
Z||000038||FIEROCIOUS||08-29-2000||02:02 AM||||WOW! Alot of people realy don't want me to do a Northstar!<P>WHY?<BR>I explained the cost, the power, the ease. Am I realy being that unreasonable?<BR>I the ability to make my own kit and what I can't do I can get so what is the problem?<P>Like bbbad said if don't make heat it ain't work'n! The Rotrex unit RSmotorsports sales is a quality unit made in Australia you can get them direct from EPI tunning for 1500.00<BR>For the price of RSmotorsports kit I can get an Engine, Tranny, and PCM!<P>Terryk, what do YOU think? Am I nutz? Should I do the Northstar or settle for the SC3800?||||reg||1
Z||000039||terryk||08-29-2000||12:00 PM||||No, I don't think you are nuts. Well, you could still be nuts, but that's not the issue here.<P>I think it depends on your aptitude. Either swap will require essentially the same work. Mounts (although the 4T60E can use some existing mounts), the exhaust, wiring, axles, etc.<P>If you plan on using a manual transmission, don't do the NS. Use either the 4T80E or don't do the swap. Same for the SC engines. Sorry guys, the code just don't like manual transmissions (even with substantial hacking). 92 SC code is a bit friendlier.<P>Franky, the NS commands to much money for the extra power they provide. It better be a damn good price for the engine/trans (&lt;$2000 and under 50k miles). <P>So, cost ok? Same effort? Automatic trans? Sure, I'd do the NS. <P>Costs more? Do the SC.<P>Want a manual trans? Do the DOHC or 3.4L upgrade.<P>IMUHO....||||reg||1
Z||000040||elfiero||08-29-2000||01:47 PM||||I'm with Mike Lecompte,for STREET USE-nothing<BR>beats a SBC! Once again,remember;<BR>  1.Size does matter<BR>  2.the more inches you got,the better you<BR>    get treated<BR>  3.torque matters-nothing else.<BR>  4.caddys are for old pathetic grandparents,<BR>    maybe thats why its almost impossible to<BR>    make them go fast.<BR>  5.If you respring your car after you put in<BR>    the V8,the average driver on the street<BR>    could'nt tell if it did'nt handle good.<BR>    <BR>    <BR>   <BR>||||reg||6
